And the selected_ptr pick (FORK 12781-12783) becomes three-way:
```c
const int32_t *selected_ptr =
    use_stream  ? g_stream_selected_cache[compact_dev].slot_selected_ptr :
    use_compact ? g_moe_compact_selected_dev[compact_dev] :
                  (const int32_t *)selected->ptr;
```
KEEP the entire existing g_moe_compact_* allocate+fill as the non-streaming else (it is the `!use_stream` arm above + the alloc block at 12646-12676, which stays unconditional — it is harmless to keep the compact scratch allocated even in streaming mode, or guard it with `if (!g_ssd_streaming_mode)` to save VRAM; I recommend the guard).

### SEAM 1 — PER-DEVICE (the must-fix correctness bug)
Upstream's `g_stream_selected_cache`, `g_stream_expert_cache`, and ALL the runtime-cap/budget statics (UP 194-200) are SINGLE globals. On our 7-GPU PP, layer L runs on device d=L%7 (or per the PP map); a single global cache holds device-d pointers, and the NEXT layer on device d' would (a) free device-d's cudaMalloc'd buffers while a graph on d still references them, and (b) hand device-d' a pointer that belongs to device-d's context → illegal-address fault or silent cross-device corruption. This is exactly why the fork already made g_moe_compact_* a [DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES] array.

FIX: when grafting the upstream statics, change to arrays indexed by compact_dev (the value already computed at FORK 12627-12629 via cudaGetDevice, clamped to [0,DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES)):
- `static cuda_stream_selected_cache g_stream_selected_cache[DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES];`
- `static cuda_stream_expert_cache  g_stream_expert_cache[DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES];`
- `static uint32_t g_stream_expert_runtime_cap[DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES];` and the other runtime_* (gate_bytes/down_bytes/memory_cap_notice) likewise. `g_stream_expert_budget_override` and `g_ssd_streaming_mode` are policy-level (same on every GPU) → keep as scalars.
Every grafted helper that touches these globals must take/derive a `dev` and index `[dev]`: `cuda_stream_selected_cache_invalidate`, `cuda_stream_expert_cache_prepare` (UP 1820), `_find/_lru_slot/_load_slot/_copy_to_compact`, `cuda_stream_selected_cache_begin_compact_load` (UP 2875), and the public `prepare_selected_batch/seed_selected/seed_experts`. Simplest mechanical rule: at the TOP of each of these, do `int dev=0; cudaGetDevice(&dev); if(dev<0||dev>=DS4_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES)dev=0;` and replace `g_stream_selected_cache.` → `g_stream_selected_cache[dev].` and `g_stream_expert_cache` → `g_stream_expert_cache[dev]` (the .slots std::vector lives per element — fine). The budget/seed config wiring in ds4.c (SPEC step 5) must LOOP over devices: `for(d=0;d<n_dev;d++){cudaSetDevice(d); ds4_gpu_set_streaming_expert_cache_budget(...); seed...;}` — otherwise only device 0 is warmed and devices 1-6 cold-stream every token.
FAILURE MODE if mishandled: intermittent `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` on the down/gate kernels for layers not on GPU0, or — worse — no error but wrong experts read (a buffer from another GPU's address space that happens to be mapped), i.e. silent garbage logits. Hard to debug because GPU0's layers look perfect.

### SEAM 2 — CUDA GRAPH CAPTURE (the subtle one)
The fork's capture-active fast path (FORK 12686-12690) sets gate_w/up_w/down_w to `g_moe_compact_*[dev]` and does NO host work, because: the H2D fill + D2H selected-read happened on a PRIOR non-captured pass that left the buffer populated, and the captured graph only bakes (i) the fixed buffer POINTER and (ii) the kernel launch params. Replays reload buffer contents out-of-band. The streaming gather CANNOT run during capture: `prepare_selected_batch` does a host D2H (implicitly via the host_selected read I do before it), host std::vector LRU bookkeeping, cudaMalloc on miss-grow, and cudaMemcpy (non-async) of slot_ids (UP 3071) — every one of these is illegal inside cudaStreamBeginCapture and aborts the capture.

CONCRETE SCHEME (pre-seed-before-replay, mirroring what the compact path already relies on):
1. In the capture_active branch, do NOT call the streaming gather. Instead point at the streaming buffers that a prior non-captured prime pass populated:
```c
if (capture_active) {
    use_compact = 1;
    if (g_ssd_streaming_mode && g_stream_selected_cache[compact_dev].valid) {
        use_stream = 1;
        gate_w = g_stream_selected_cache[compact_dev].gate_ptr;
        up_w   = g_stream_selected_cache[compact_dev].up_ptr;
        down_w = g_stream_selected_cache[compact_dev].down_ptr;
    } else { gate_w = g_moe_compact_gate[compact_dev]; ... }
}
```
2. POINTER STABILITY is the precondition. `cuda_stream_selected_ensure_bytes` (UP 1763) only reallocs when bytes>capacity. For decode n_tokens=1 the compact_count is ≤6 and monotonic-capped, so after the first prime pass the pointer is stable and safe to bake. To GUARANTEE it: in streaming mode size the selected cache to the WORST case (compact_count = min(n_total_expert, n_tokens*n_expert)) on the prime pass so capacity never grows post-capture — add a one-time `prepare` with a full-6-unique selection during warmup, or force `cuda_stream_selected_ensure_bytes` to `n_expert*n_tokens*expert_bytes` (not the dedup count) so the allocation is capture-invariant.
3. THE DATA-CONTENT REPLAY PROBLEM (this is the real teeth): unlike the compact path whose buffer is refilled by a kernel/patch inside replay, the streaming buffer is filled by HOST-side LRU copies that DON'T run during graph replay. A captured+replayed graph would read STALE expert weights from the prior step. Therefore: for streamed layers, the streaming gather (prepare_selected_batch) MUST execute on the decode stream OUTSIDE capture, immediately before `ds4_gpu_decode_graph_launch`/`pp_chunk_graph_launch`, every decode step. Two valid implementations:
   (a) PREFERRED — DISABLE graph capture for layers whose experts are streamed. Gate `cuda_decode_graph_enabled()`/the subgraph capture (FORK ds4_gpu_decode_subgraph_capture ~263, pp_chunk_graph_capture_begin 304) with `&& !(g_ssd_streaming_mode)` for MoE-bearing layers. The launch/D2H cost the graph was hiding is dwarfed by the SSD/RAM→HBM expert copy in streaming mode anyway (per DS4 decode-bottleneck memory, decode is launch-bound at ~25 t/s on the resident model; streaming adds copy latency that makes graph savings negligible). This is the safe, correct default — ship this first.
   (b) ADVANCED — keep the graph but split the step: run `prepare_selected_batch` for ALL streamed layers on the decode stream (non-captured) to pre-seed every per-layer g_stream_selected_cache, THEN replay the graph. This requires per-layer selected caches (not one per device) so layer L's buffer isn't overwritten by layer L+1 before replay — i.e. g_stream_selected_cache becomes [dev][layer], a large VRAM cost (6 experts * ~bytes * n_layers_on_dev). Only worth it if (a) proves too slow. Document as future work.
FAILURE MODE if mishandled: if the gather runs during capture → `cudaErrorStreamCaptureUnsupported`, capture aborts, decode silently falls back to per-step (or hangs in PP if the abort path isn't clean). If capture succeeds but the host fill is skipped on replay → CORRECT-LOOKING run with WRONG logits (reads last step's experts), the nastiest failure: no crash, just quality collapse that looks like a quant bug.

### SEAM 3 — layer-index plumbing gap (NEW, I found this; flag for orchestrator)
The streaming table needs `table.layer` to key the GPU-LRU per layer. But the DECODE entry `ds4_gpu_routed_moe_one_tensor` (FORK 13311) does NOT receive a layer index, and the batch entry `ds4_gpu_routed_moe_batch_tensor` (13320) receives `layer_index` but `(void)`-discards it (13321). routed_moe_launch has no layer param. Without a real layer id, every layer's experts collide in the LRU under layer=0 → the cache treats expert 5 of layer 3 and expert 5 of layer 40 as the same slot → massive false-hit corruption (returns wrong layer's weights). FIX: thread a layer id into routed_moe_launch — either add a param (touches 2 call sites + both extern entry signatures, which ds4.c calls) OR set a file-scope `static __thread uint32_t g_routed_moe_cur_layer;` from the batch entry's layer_index and from a new layer arg on the one-tensor entry. The seed-experts hotlist path needs the same id. This MUST be resolved or the LRU is unusable; recommend the explicit param to avoid TLS-vs-PP-thread surprises.

